  data: {
    LLM: "Watch the upcards carefully. If you see the Ace, King, and Queen of Spades all dealt face up, your Jack of Spades in the hole becomes much more valuable. Conversely, if high spades are showing, your hole spade is less likely to win.\n\nBalanced Play: If you have a strong poker hand but no spade in the hole, you can only win half the pot. Consider whether your hand is strong enough to justify playing for half. If you have a weak hand but the Ace of Spades in the hole, you have a lock on half the pot and should stay in.\n\nDeclaration Strategy: In simultaneous declaration games, watch your opponents' betting patterns. Heavy betting often indicates a strong poker hand; cautious play with many spades showing might indicate someone hunting for the spade half. If you have both a strong hand AND a high spade, declaring 'Both' can scoop the entire pot—but you must win both halves.",
    Player: "Watch the upcards carefully. If you see the Ace, King, and Queen of Spades all dealt face up, your Jack of Spades in the hole becomes much more valuable. Conversely, if high spades are showing, your hole spade is less likely to win.\n\nBalanced Play: If you have a strong poker hand but no spade in the hole, you can only win half the pot. Consider whether your hand is strong enough to justify playing for half. If you have a weak hand but the Ace of Spades in the hole, you have a lock on half the pot and should stay in.\n\nDeclaration Strategy: In simultaneous declaration games, watch your opponents' betting patterns. Heavy betting often indicates a strong poker hand; cautious play with many spades showing might indicate someone hunting for the spade half. If you have both a strong hand AND a high spade, declaring 'Both' can scoop the entire pot—but you must win both halves.",
