    basic: 'Watch the upcards carefully. If you see the Ace, King, and Queen of Spades all dealt face up, your Jack of Spades in the hole becomes much more valuable. Conversely, if high spades are showing, your hole spade is less likely to win.',
    intermediate: 'Balanced Play: If you have a strong poker hand but no spade in the hole, you can only win half the pot. Consider whether your hand is strong enough to justify playing for half. If you have a weak hand but the Ace of Spades in the hole, you have a lock on half the pot and should stay in.',
    advanced: "Declaration Strategy: In simultaneous declaration games, watch your opponents' betting patterns. Heavy betting often indicates a strong poker hand; cautious play with many spades showing might indicate someone hunting for the spade half. If you have both a strong hand AND a high spade, declaring 'Both' can scoop the entire pot—but you must win both halves.",
    tips: [
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: 'Track all spades: Count how many spades are visible and which ranks remain possible in holes.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "The Ace of Spades in the hole is the 'nuts' for the spade half—it's unbeatable.",
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: 'If four or more spades are showing on board, the remaining spades in holes are more valuable.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "Don't overvalue medium-strength poker hands when you have no spade—you can only win half.",
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: 'In Low Chicago, the Two of Spades in the hole is the nuts (aces count high).',
